        "heading": "Understanding overlapping pressures",
        "content": "Social challenges are best understood through the resources people can use and the risks they face. Iran’s experience illustrates why economic output alone is an incomplete guide to household welfare. The World Bank’s poverty diagnostic describes uneven gains during periods of expansion and substantial losses during downturns. It also identifies the pandemic’s disproportionate employment effects on women, including mothers carrying additional caregiving responsibilities. These findings concern a particular historical period; they should not be treated as a measurement of every household’s present circumstances. [s1]\n\nA useful distinction runs through this section: poverty concerns resources relative to a defined threshold, inequality concerns their distribution, and vulnerability concerns the risk of future hardship. A household above a poverty line can still face considerable insecurity. The diagnostic explicitly examines vulnerability as well as measured poverty, making this distinction central to understanding social change. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "housing-and-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where people live changes the problem",
        "content": "Housing connects household budgets to the geography of opportunity. The European Union Agency for Asylum’s January 2025 assessment reports that high housing costs and weakened purchasing power have encouraged some people to leave Tehran for surrounding towns. It also describes sanitation and service difficulties in disadvantaged settlements. These observations establish localized pressures, rather than a uniform picture of the capital or the country. [s2]\n\nEmployment is another part of this geography of disadvantage. The same assessment identifies particularly difficult labor-market conditions for women, young people, and university graduates. Reading housing and employment together raises a practical question: does a cheaper location also offer realistic access to work and essential services? The answer requires local evidence, rather than assumptions based on rent alone. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coordinated-responses",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Responses cross institutional boundaries",
        "content": "The United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework for Iran provides one example of an explicitly connected approach. Developed jointly with the government for 2023–2027, it identifies socioeconomic resilience, public health, environmental protection, disaster risk management, and the impacts of drug use and trafficking as priorities. This is a statement of intended cooperation, not an independent evaluation of results. [s3]\n\nThe One UN Impact Fund is designed to pool donor resources around that framework and support joint programming. Its stated objectives include coordinating resources and allowing responses to sudden emergencies. The distinction between a funding mechanism’s objectives, its actual expenditure, and demonstrated improvements in people’s lives matters: the existence of a fund does not establish effective coverage or equitable access. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "current-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading current conditions cautiously",
        "content": "The World Bank’s October 2026 poverty brief describes conflict-related economic stress and risks to household welfare. Its discussion includes projections, which must remain distinguishable from completed household surveys. [s5]\n\nThe pages in this branch therefore separate measurement from interpretation. Poverty and housing provide concrete starting points; social justice, public-service access, social vulnerabilities, and community empowerment require additional evidence about rights, implementation, participation, and lived outcomes. A credible account should recognize differences within Iran while resisting the idea that a single statistic can summarize social experience.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
