    "description": "Poverty and inequality describe different aspects of household welfare in Iran: resources below a defined threshold and differences across the distribution. Household expenditure surveys support analysis, but poverty lines, price adjustments, survey periods, and projections must remain clearly distinguished.",
    "tags": ["Poverty", "Inequality", "Household expenditure", "Social protection", "Regional disparities", "Purchasing power"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:35:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:35:23Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Poverty and Inequality in Iran: Measures, Trends and Limits",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s household poverty evidence, regional disparities, expenditure-based measures, and the limits of comparing different poverty lines."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "definitions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The poverty line changes the question",
        "content": "A poverty rate is meaningful only alongside its threshold and method. The World Bank’s October 2026 brief reports different headcounts for its international poverty lines. These are purchasing-power-parity benchmarks: they compare purchasing power across economies rather than translating household resources at a market exchange rate. The brief’s welfare measure uses household expenditure per person with adjustments for differences in prices over time and place. It should not be described simply as reported cash income. [s1]\n\nInequality asks a different question: how unevenly resources are distributed. A poverty headcount can decline without a similarly large change in inequality. Neither measure alone describes wealth ownership, financial resilience, or every form of exclusion.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A setback followed by a partial recovery",
        "content": "The World Bank’s 2023 diagnostic describes worsening household welfare during the preceding decade, with poorer households benefiting less from expansions and suffering heavily during recessions. That finding concerns the distribution of economic change, rather than simply the national growth rate. Its historical estimates should be read within that publication’s methodology. [s2]\n\nA subsequent Economic Monitor found falling poverty during the recovery covered by the annual observations below. It associated improvements with earnings and social transfers, including temporary transfer top-ups. Its decomposition is an analytical attribution of changes in measured welfare, not experimental proof that one policy caused the entire improvement. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-poverty-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: historical poverty at $6.85 a day in 2017 PPP",
          "dataset_id": "historical-poverty-2017-ppp",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["poverty-headcount"],
          "x_axis_label": "Original reporting-year label",
          "y_axis_label": "Population below the threshold (%)",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The source-estimated headcount declines across all three reporting periods. This historical series must not be extended using the newer $8.30 threshold in 2021 PPP."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "unequal-exposure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "National averages conceal differences",
        "content": "The October 2026 brief’s survey-based subgroup table shows higher poverty among rural residents than urban residents, and among children than older adults, at the same upper-middle-income benchmark. These are associations, not explanations of individual outcomes. Household composition, education, location, and employment conditions need to be examined together. [s1]\n\nRegional disparities also appear in the earlier Economic Monitor, which highlights the southeast. Changes in the national average therefore cannot establish that every province or social group experienced equivalent progress. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "latest-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Survey findings and the outlook are different evidence",
        "content": "The latest inspected brief combines survey-based results, a preliminary estimate for a later reporting period, and an outlook under conflict-related uncertainty. The outlook is not a completed survey of current living standards. [s1]\n\nThe most useful reading of Iran’s poverty evidence consequently preserves the original threshold, price basis, and reporting period. It also asks whether support protects purchasing power over time. A nominal payment and the goods it can buy are different quantities; the historical diagnostic identifies inflation-related erosion of transfers as an important limitation of social protection. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
