    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Housing access in Iran involves the ability to obtain a home in a workable location with dependable services and acceptable physical safety. Evidence from Tehran and national urban-planning documents shows why affordability, neighborhood conditions, and resilience need to be examined together.",
    "tags": ["Housing access", "Affordability", "Tehran", "Informal settlements", "Urban services", "Earthquake resilience"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:35:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:35:23Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Housing Access in Iran: Affordability, Location and Safety",
      "description": "Examine housing pressures in Iran through affordability, metropolitan relocation, neighborhood services, and earthquake resilience."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "access-and-location",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Affordability has a geography",
        "content": "Finding housing is also a question of where a household can live. The European Union Agency for Asylum’s January 2025 assessment describes housing as largely market-driven and reports movement from Tehran to smaller surrounding towns in response to high costs and declining purchasing power. This is evidence of metropolitan displacement pressure, not a national count of households unable to obtain housing. [s1]\n\nA move to a cheaper location should therefore be assessed alongside access to work, education, health care, and transport. Lower rent alone cannot establish whether a household’s overall situation has improved. The available assessment does not measure those trade-offs systematically, leaving an important gap between evidence of relocation and evidence of its consequences. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "neighborhood-conditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different kinds of vulnerable neighborhoods",
        "content": "UN-Habitat’s country programme distinguishes dilapidated urban areas, informal settlements, and historic urban fabric within its discussion of regeneration needs. These categories should not be treated as interchangeable. A historic neighborhood is not necessarily an informal settlement, and an area identified for regeneration is not automatically a count of homelessness. [s2]\n\nThe programme places housing within a wider urban system, connecting upgrading with basic services and community participation. This framing matters because a dwelling’s usefulness depends partly on its surroundings. Its proposals, however, establish intended activity rather than verified improvements for residents. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "physical-safety",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Physical safety is part of access",
        "content": "Research in Farahzad, an informal settlement in Tehran, illustrates a dimension of housing disadvantage that prices cannot capture. A cross-sectional study assessed residential structures using rapid visual seismic screening and interviewed residents about preparedness and household characteristics. Its published abstract reports serious construction and preparedness concerns in the surveyed setting. [s3]\n\nThe study supports attention to structural safety alongside affordability. It does not establish the condition of all housing in Tehran, predict a particular earthquake, or provide a current inspection of individual buildings. Its local scope and historical fieldwork are essential to interpreting its findings responsibly. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "policy-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Plans need outcome evidence",
        "content": "UN-Habitat’s programme identifies affordable-housing planning, upgrading vulnerable urban areas, and metropolitan governance as related priorities. Its account names the Ministry of Roads and Urban Development as its national counterpart. These relationships explain a programme’s institutional setting; they do not establish that housing has become affordable or that proposed interventions have been completed. [s2]\n\nA fuller assessment needs comparable household-budget and rent evidence, distinctions between owners and renters, and documented results from housing programmes. Until those are verified, precise national affordability claims would exceed the evidence assembled here. The supported conclusion is narrower: housing access must be evaluated through costs, location, services, and safety together.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Country Guidance: Iran — 6.4.1. General situation",
        "publisher": "European Union Agency for Asylum",
